WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.200
Today: embeddings and semantic search, the technology that lets machines understand meaning, not keywords.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:13.400
An embedding is a list of numbers, usually 384 to 3072 long, that represents what a text means.

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:20.700
Texts with similar meanings land close together in embedding space, even with zero shared words.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:27.900
That's why how do I reset my password matches with password recovery help, but not with keyword search.

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:35.100
Similarity is computed with cosine similarity, the cosine of the angle between the two vectors.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:42.080
A score of one means identical direction, zero means unrelated, negative means opposite meaning.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:49.300
Choosing an embedding model, look at the MTEB leaderboard on Hugging Face for benchmarks on real tasks.

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:56.400
Dimensions affect storage and speed. Smaller models with 384 dimensions are often good enough.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:03.700
Normalized embeddings let you use dot product as cosine similarity, which is faster in practice.

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:10.900
Beware: embedding models are trained on specific kinds of text. A code model won't do well on poetry.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:18.200
When you switch embedding models, you must re-embed your entire corpus. Plan for that migration cost.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:25.400
Chunk-level embeddings lose context. Prefixing chunks with document titles helps retrieval quality a lot.

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:32.600
Semantic search evaluation: build pairs of queries and known relevant documents, then measure recall.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:40.000
That golden evaluation set is your ground truth for every future improvement you experiment with.
